---
/**
 * LEGACY TIMELINE — the four milestones of Sunbeam Ballia, as alternating cards
 * on a stepped dotted path.
 *
 * ═══ WHY THIS EXISTS WHEN JourneyRoad ALREADY DID ═══════════════════════════
 *
 * JourneyRoad is a zigzag of TEXT: a numbered disc, a rolling title and a
 * paragraph, with dotted arcs between. It is still the right component for the
 * sequences it serves — Assessment & Support runs on it and nothing here
 * replaces it.
 *
 * What it cannot do is carry a picture, and that is the whole brief for this
 * page. A legacy read as four paragraphs in a row is an argument; read as four
 * illustrated cards it is a school. So this is a card component with an image
 * bonded to it, not a variant of the road — a `picture?` prop bolted onto
 * JourneyRoad would have put an optional 40%-width media column into a layout
 * whose three consumers do not want one, and every one of them would then be
 * paying for the branch.
 *
 * ⚠ IT REPLACES THE ROAD ON THIS PAGE ONLY. about/history-legacy.astro no longer
 * imports JourneyRoad; every other consumer is untouched.
 *
 * ═══ THE HEADING THIS REPLACED WAS ALSO WRONG ═══════════════════════════════
 *
 * ⚠ "Fifty years, and thirteen in Ballia" IS GONE, AND NOT ONLY BECAUSE THE
 * CLIENT SUPPLIED A NEW HEADING. 1972 to 2026 is fifty-FOUR years. The line was
 * written when the site was, and it aged into a false claim sitting directly
 * above a timeline that starts with the correct year — a reader could do the
 * subtraction from the card underneath it. The replacement, "An Unchallenged
 * Legacy of Excellence", carries no arithmetic and so cannot go stale.
 *
 * ⚠ AND THAT IS WHY NOTHING BELOW COUNTS YEARS EITHER. The four cards state
 * 1972, 2013, "Today" and "Six years" — every one of them either a fixed date or
 * a figure the school itself publishes and maintains. No elapsed total is
 * computed, printed, or implied.
 *
 * ═══ THE ARTWORK ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * The four sepia pencil drawings — Varanasi ghats, campus, students, trophy —
 * live in src/assets/icons/ and are passed in by the page, not imported here.
 * That is what keeps this component a layout rather than this page's timeline.
 *
 * ⚠⚠ THERE IS NO `filter: sepia()` ANYWHERE IN THIS FILE AND THERE MUST NOT BE.
 * The drawings ARE sepia — ink and wash on cream, already the finished
 * treatment. A CSS filter on top would apply it twice and turn all four orange.
 * The tone in the cards comes from the artwork itself; nothing here touches it.
 *
 * ⚠ AND THE MEDIA PANEL'S BACKGROUND IS CREAM, NOT GREY, FOR THE SAME REASON.
 * The drawings' own ground is a warm cream, so any strip of panel showing beyond
 * the artwork at an unexpected aspect ratio has to be that cream or the card
 * grows a visible seam down the middle of its own picture.
 *
 * ═══ LAYOUT ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
 *
 * Alternating left/right. The illustration is FLUSH TO THE CARD EDGE — no inner
 * padding, no gap, no inset radius on the bonded corners — so the picture reads
 * as part of the card rather than as a picture placed on one. The number badge
 * floats on the seam between the two halves, which is what visually stitches
 * them; that is why it is positioned against the seam and not against the card.
 *
 * ⚠ THE MEDIA COLUMN'S WIDTH IS ONE CUSTOM PROPERTY, `--lg-media`, AND IT HAS TO
 * BE. It sets the grid track AND the badge's offset from the seam, and those two
 * are the same measurement — hard-code the number twice and the next person to
 * widen the picture leaves the badge floating in the middle of it.
 *
 * The connectors are STEPPED, not arcs: each leaves the lower corner of one card,
 * runs out into the gutter, drops, and runs on into the top corner of the next.
 * An arc implies a smooth interval; between 1972 and 2013 there is nothing
 * smooth. Dotted, for the reason JourneyRoad gives — a solid rule implies the
 * gaps are to scale, and these are forty-one years, thirteen years and six.
 *
 * ⚠ THE CONNECTOR PAINTS UNDER THE CARDS, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES IT LOOK DRAWN
 * TO THE EDGES. It is wider than the gutter it crosses, so its ends run beneath
 * the two cards and the dots simply emerge from under each card's edge. Trying
 * to size it to the gutter exactly instead means re-deriving that width at every
 * breakpoint and shipping a one-pixel gap between the line and the card at most
 * of them.
 *
 * ⚠ THE CONNECTORS ARE INLINE SVG AND ARE `aria-hidden`, AND THEY ARE ALSO NOT
 * IN THE MOBILE LAYOUT. Under 900px the cards stack, so a stepped path between
 * them would have to be redrawn at every width; the mobile treatment is a single
 * vertical dotted rail with the badges sitting ON it, which needs no geometry.
 * The SVGs are `display: none` there rather than rendered and hidden, so they
 * cost nothing.
 *
 * ⚠ THE BACKGROUND IS DRAWN IN CSS, NOT LOADED. A heritage ground at under 10%
 * opacity is one repeating linear-gradient; shipping an image for something
 * invisible at full strength is bytes spent on nothing, and a texture PNG cannot
 * follow the section's own colour tokens the way a gradient can.
 *
 * ⚠ THE WATERMARK IS REAL TEXT WITH aria-hidden, NOT AN <img> AND NOT A
 * PSEUDO-ELEMENT'S content STRING. As text it scales with the viewport and
 * inherits the display face; aria-hidden because "SUNBEAM BALLIA" read aloud
 * between two milestones is noise, and the school's name is already the <h1> of
 * every page on the site.
 */
import { Picture } from 'astro:assets';
import type { ImageMetadata } from 'astro';

export interface Milestone {
  /** The large line — a year, or a span. */
  year: string;
  /** The small-caps line under it. */
  kicker: string;
  body: string;
  /** Flush to the card edge. Omitted renders a text-only card, not a hole. */
  art?: ImageMetadata;
  /** Required whenever `art` is given. */
  artAlt?: string;
}

interface Props {
  eyebrow?: string;
  heading: string;
  /** The word inside `heading` that takes the accent plate. Must occur in it. */
  headingAccent?: string;
  standfirst?: string;
  items: readonly Milestone[];
  /** Spoken name for the list. */
  label?: string;
  watermark?: string;
}

const {
  eyebrow = 'Our Legacy',
  heading,
  headingAccent,
  standfirst,
  items,
  label = 'Milestones',
  watermark = 'Sunbeam Ballia',
} = Astro.props as Props;

/**
 * The heading is split around the accented word rather than being passed in as
 * markup, so the page states its copy as a plain string and cannot ship an
 * unbalanced tag. If `headingAccent` is absent or is not in the heading, the
 * whole line renders unaccented — a missing plate, never a missing heading.
 */
const cut = headingAccent ? heading.indexOf(headingAccent) : -1;
const head =
  cut === -1
    ? { before: heading, accent: '', after: '' }
    : {
        before: heading.slice(0, cut),
        accent: headingAccent!,
        after: heading.slice(cut + headingAccent!.length),
      };
---

<section class="lg" aria-labelledby="lg-heading">
  {/* The heritage ground and the watermark. Both decorative, both out of the
      accessibility tree, both behind everything via z-index on the content. */}
  <span class="lg__ground" aria-hidden="true"></span>
  <span class="lg__watermark" aria-hidden="true">{watermark}</span>

  {/* ⚠ u-container-WIDE (1440), NOT u-container (1280). Two cards side by side
      with a gutter between them is the widest thing on this page — at 1280 each
      card is 614px and the four-line paragraphs inside them run to six lines,
      which is what makes the column look cramped next to its own picture. The
      wide container is an existing token, not a number invented here. */}
  <div class="u-container-wide lg__inner">
    <header class="lg__head" data-reveal>
      {eyebrow && <p class="lg__eyebrow t-label">{eyebrow}</p>}
      <h2 class="lg__heading t-h2-sans" id="lg-heading" data-split>
        {head.before}{head.accent && <span class="t-squiggle">{head.accent}</span>}{head.after}
      </h2>
      {standfirst && <p class="lg__stand">{standfirst}</p>}
    </header>

    <ol class="lg__list" aria-label={label}>
      {items.map((m, i) => (
        <li class:list={['lg__step', i % 2 === 1 && 'lg__step--right']} data-reveal>
          <article class="lg__card">
            {m.art && (
              <div class="lg__media">
                <Picture
                  src={m.art}
                  alt={m.artAlt ?? ''}
                  formats={['avif', 'webp']}
                  widths={[360, 560, 720]}
                  sizes="(max-width: 899px) 100vw, 300px"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
              </div>
            )}

            {/* ⚠ THE BADGE IS A CHILD OF THE CARD, NOT OF THE MEDIA. On the
                seam it must sit above both halves; inside .lg__media it would be
                clipped by that element's overflow:hidden, which is what keeps
                the picture inside the bonded corners. */}
            <span class="lg__badge" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>

            <div class="lg__body">
              <p class="lg__year">{m.year}</p>
              <p class="lg__kicker">{m.kicker}</p>
              <p class="lg__text">{m.body}</p>
            </div>
          </article>

          {/* Stepped dotted connector into the next card. The last step has
              none — a path leading out of the final milestone would promise a
              fifth.

              ⚠ preserveAspectRatio="none" IS DELIBERATE. The box stretches to
              whatever the gutter is at this width, so the two horizontal runs
              stretch with it while the drop does not — which is the behaviour
              wanted, since the hop gets wider on a wide screen and no taller.
              Uniform scaling would shrink the whole step instead and leave both
              ends short of the cards they are supposed to run under.

              ⚠ ONE PATH, NOT THREE SEGMENTS. Three would restart the dash
              pattern at each corner and put a double-length gap on the inside of
              both bends. */}
          {i < items.length - 1 && (
            <svg
              class="lg__link"
              viewBox="0 0 160 116"
              fill="none"
              preserveAspectRatio="none"
              aria-hidden="true"
              focusable="false"
            >
              <path
                d="M0 14 H68 Q80 14 80 26 V90 Q80 102 92 102 H160"
                stroke="currentColor"
                stroke-width="2.5"
                stroke-linecap="round"
                stroke-linejoin="round"
                stroke-dasharray="1 10"
              />
            </svg>
          )}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .lg {
    position: relative;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
    padding-block: var(--sb-section-y);
    background: var(--sb-sand);
  }

  /* --- Heritage ground -------------------------------------------------------
     Two layers: a wide dot field and a fine grid, both at a fraction of full
     strength. `opacity` on the layer rather than alpha in each gradient, so the
     whole ground has ONE number to turn down and it is visibly under 10%. */
  .lg__ground {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 0;
    opacity: 0.075;
    pointer-events: none;
    /* ⚠ VERTICAL RULES ONLY, NOT A GRID. A full grid put a horizontal line
       across the gutter at the exact height the dotted connectors cross it, and
       at 7% the two were indistinguishable — the path read as broken. Uprights
       alone give the ground its texture and never run parallel to a connector. */
    background-image: linear-gradient(90deg, var(--sb-violet) 1px, transparent 1px);
    background-size: 46px 100%;
  }

  /* Sits behind the cards and above the ground. `clamp` on a vw-driven size so
     it is a watermark at every width rather than a headline on a phone. */
  /* ⚠ BOTTOM-LEFT AND DELIBERATELY RUNNING OFF THE EDGE. A watermark centred and
     fully legible is a heading nobody asked for; cropped by the section's own
     `overflow: hidden` it reads as a ground the cards are sitting on. The
     negative offset is what does the cropping, and it is safe precisely because
     the section clips — remove that `overflow: hidden` and this gives the page a
     horizontal scrollbar.

     ⚠ IT WRAPS ONTO TWO LINES BY MEASURE, NOT BY A <br>. `max-width: 5.2em` at
     this size breaks it after "SUNBEAM" whatever the viewport is doing, and the
     prop stays a plain string the page can change to anything. */
  .lg__watermark {
    position: absolute;
    /* Far enough left that the first letter of each line is cut by the
       section's own clip — a watermark that is fully legible has stopped being
       a watermark and started being a heading nobody asked for. */
    left: clamp(-80px, -4.5vw, -24px);
    bottom: clamp(-20px, -1.2vw, 0px);
    z-index: 0;
    max-width: 5.2em;
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: clamp(52px, 8.5vw, 150px);
    line-height: 0.84;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
    opacity: 0.055;
    pointer-events: none;
    user-select: none;
  }

  .lg__inner {
    position: relative;
    z-index: 1;
  }

  /* --- Head ---------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .lg__head {
    max-width: 62ch;
    margin: 0 auto clamp(48px, 7vh, 84px);
    text-align: center;
  }

  .lg__eyebrow {
    color: var(--sb-violet);
    margin-bottom: var(--sb-4);
  }

  .lg__heading {
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .lg__stand {
    margin-top: var(--sb-6);
    font-size: var(--sb-body-lg, 17px);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--sb-text-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  /* --- The path ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .lg__list {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
  }

  /* Each step is a full-width row holding a half-width card. The card takes the
     left half or the right half; the centre line the connectors run down is the
     row's own midpoint, so no absolute rail element is needed. */
  .lg__step {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: flex-start;
    /* The width of the picture panel, and therefore the badge's distance from
       the card's outer edge. One number, two rules — see the header. */
    --lg-media: clamp(140px, 13vw, 200px);
  }

  .lg__step--right {
    justify-content: flex-end;
  }

  /* --- The card ------------------------------------------------------------- */

  .lg__card {
    position: relative;
    /* Above the connectors, which is what hides their overhanging ends. */
    z-index: 1;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: var(--lg-media) minmax(0, 1fr);
    align-items: stretch;
    /* 48%, not a pixel width: the 4% left over IS the gutter the connectors
       cross, so it stays proportional at every width instead of collapsing to
       nothing on a small laptop and yawning open on a wide monitor. */
    width: 48%;
    background: var(--sb-paper);
    border: var(--sb-hairline);
    border-radius: var(--sb-r-2);
    box-shadow: var(--sb-shadow-card);
    /* ⚠ THE CLIP IS WHAT MAKES THE PICTURE "FLUSH". The media has no radius of
       its own; the CARD's radius crops it. Give .lg__media its own corners
       instead and the two curves disagree by a pixel at every zoom level. */
    overflow: hidden;
  }

  /* Odd steps put the picture on the outside edge, so the two columns mirror. */
  .lg__step--right .lg__card {
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) var(--lg-media);
  }

  .lg__step--right .lg__media {
    order: 2;
  }

  /* Cream, not grey — the drawings' own ground, so no seam shows where the
     artwork ends. See the header. */
  .lg__media {
    position: relative;
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #f7efe4;
  }

  /* Stretched to the card's full height — the picture is bonded to the card, so
     it ends where the card ends and not where the photograph would prefer to. */
  /* ⚠⚠ THE <picture> WRAPPER HAS TO BE SIZED TOO, NOT JUST THE <img> INSIDE IT.
     <Picture> emits `<picture><source…><img></picture>`, and <picture> is an
     INLINE box that shrinks to its image's natural aspect. So `height: 100%` on
     the img resolved against a wrapper that was only as tall as the drawing, the
     grid stretched the cream panel to the card's full height underneath it, and
     every card carried a block of empty cream below its artwork. The img rule
     was doing exactly what it said; it was measuring against the wrong box.

     ⚠ BOTH RULES, AND BOTH `height: 100%`. Dropping either one brings the gap
     straight back. */
  .lg__media :global(picture) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
  }

  .lg__media :global(img) {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    /* 50% 50%: these are drawn illustrations with their subject centred, not
       photographs with a face to keep out of the crop. */
    object-position: 50% 50%;
  }

  /* --- The badge on the seam ------------------------------------------------ */

  .lg__badge {
    position: absolute;
    /* The seam is the media column's inner edge — `--lg-media` in from the left
       on an even step, the same distance from the right on an odd one. Half the
       badge overhangs onto each side, which is what stitches them. */
    left: var(--lg-media);
    top: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);
    z-index: 2;
    display: grid;
    place-items: center;
    width: 48px;
    height: 48px;
    border-radius: var(--sb-r-full);
    background: var(--sb-violet);
    color: #fff;
    border: 3px solid var(--sb-paper);
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 800;
    font-size: 15px;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .lg__step--right .lg__badge {
    left: auto;
    right: var(--lg-media);
    transform: translate(50%, -50%);
  }

  /* --- The words ------------------------------------------------------------ */

  .lg__body {
    padding: clamp(22px, 2vw, 32px);
    /* Room for the badge's overhang, on whichever side it lands. */
    padding-left: clamp(40px, 3.4vw, 50px);
  }

  .lg__step--right .lg__body {
    padding-left: clamp(22px, 2vw, 32px);
    padding-right: clamp(40px, 3.4vw, 50px);
  }

  .lg__year {
    font-family: var(--sb-font-display);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: clamp(24px, 2vw, 30px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    color: var(--sb-charcoal);
  }

  .lg__kicker {
    margin-top: var(--sb-2, 6px);
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 700;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sb-violet);
  }

  .lg__text {
    margin-top: var(--sb-5);
    font-size: var(--sb-body-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--sb-text-secondary);
  }

  /* --- The connectors ------------------------------------------------------- */

  /* Spans from inside this card's lower edge to inside the next card's upper
     one, crossing the gutter between them. Both ends run UNDER a card — see the
     header — so what a reader sees is a dotted step in the gap, drawn edge to
     edge, with no join to notice. */
  .lg__link {
    position: absolute;
    left: 50%;
    top: calc(100% - 52px);
    transform: translateX(-50%);
    width: clamp(120px, 16%, 200px);
    height: 116px;
    color: var(--sb-violet);
    opacity: 0.5;
    z-index: 0;
    pointer-events: none;
  }

  /* ⚠ SMALL, ON PURPOSE. The cards alternate sides, so consecutive rows barely
     overlap horizontally and a large gap here reads as four unrelated cards
     rather than as one path. The connector is what carries the eye across, and
     it needs a short hop to cross, not a chasm. */
  .lg__step + .lg__step {
    margin-top: clamp(14px, 1.6vh, 22px);
  }

  /* ⚠ THE STEP MIRRORS ON ODD ROWS. Card 1 sits left and card 2 right, so that
     hop runs down-and-RIGHT; card 2 to card 3 runs down-and-LEFT. One path,
     flipped, rather than a second <path> to keep in sync with it. */
  .lg__step--right .lg__link {
    transform: translateX(-50%) scaleX(-1);
  }

  /* --- Under 900: one column, one rail --------------------------------------
     The stepped connector is a two-column device and there is nothing left for
     it to step between, so it goes and a single dotted rail takes over. The rail
     is a border on the LIST, not an element, and the badges sit on it. */
  @media (max-width: 899px) {
    .lg__link {
      display: none;
    }

    .lg__list {
      position: relative;
      padding-left: 34px;
      /* The rail. Dotted for the same reason the connectors were: it is a
         sequence, not a scale. */
      border-left: 2px dotted color-mix(in srgb, var(--sb-violet) 55%, transparent);
      margin-left: 22px;
    }

    .lg__step,
    .lg__step--right {
      justify-content: flex-start;
    }

    .lg__card,
    .lg__step--right .lg__card {
      width: 100%;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .lg__step--right .lg__media {
      order: 0;
    }

    .lg__media {
      aspect-ratio: 16 / 9;
    }

    /* On the rail, level with the top of the card — not the middle, because a
       badge centred on a tall stacked card floats away from the thing it
       numbers. */
    .lg__badge,
    .lg__step--right .lg__badge {
      left: -34px;
      right: auto;
      top: 26px;
      transform: translate(-50%, 0);
    }

    .lg__body,
    .lg__step--right .lg__body {
      padding: clamp(20px, 5vw, 28px);
    }

    .lg__step + .lg__step {
      margin-top: clamp(32px, 5vh, 48px);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 479px) {
    /* At this width the watermark is wider than the phone and its overflow is
       what would give the page a horizontal scrollbar. */
    .lg__watermark {
      font-size: 56px;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    .lg__card {
      transition: none;
    }
  }
</style>
